MID_SE_Tia_Set_C: <EDH>
MID_SE_Tia_Set_C_001: You are quite the reader, aren't you, Tialla? What are\nyou paging through now—history, perhaps?
MID_SE_Tia_Set_C_002: It's a tale that has been popular lately. It's a story of a\ngirl who borrows a ghost's power to fight evil.
MID_SE_Tia_Set_C_003: Really? Interesting. I'd heard that you prefer\ntopics of a more scholarly nature.
MID_SE_Tia_Set_C_004: Oh, I'll read anything and everything.
MID_SE_Tia_Set_C_005: Then maybe you'd consider reading this.
MID_SE_Tia_Set_C_006: I've never seen that one! Where did you get it?
MID_SE_Tia_Set_C_007: It's just a little something I wrote myself. I've been\ntrying my hand at children's stories.
MID_SE_Tia_Set_C_008: Dagda has such stories, of course, like one where\nbad children go into the Divine Emperor's cauldron.
MID_SE_Tia_Set_C_009: But I hope to write stories that children find less\nshocking, though they do need to hold interest...
MID_SE_Tia_Set_C_010: Right. Too scary a story will only drive\nchildren away.
MID_SE_Tia_Set_C_011: You need to draw a distinction between good and\nbad. And then write a story about avoiding the bad.
MID_SE_Tia_Set_C_012: Well, exactly so! I've tried to put pen to paper and\ndo just that with this book.
MID_SE_Tia_Set_C_013: I'll read it right now. Hold on for a while, Seteth.
MID_SE_Tia_Set_C_014: Well then, well then? Tell me what you think.\nHold nothing back.
MID_SE_Tia_Set_C_015: You know, the story was good overall, but I had an\nissue with the ending.
MID_SE_Tia_Set_C_016: The main-character yarc knew it should share with\nthe other yarcs, but it still ate all the grass by itself.
MID_SE_Tia_Set_C_017: But it was just one time. And then it gets struck by\nlightning? Isn't the punishment overly cruel?
MID_SE_Tia_Set_C_018: The plot made it clear that the animal knew its\nfriends would starve. And still he glutted!
MID_SE_Tia_Set_C_019: Life may be more ambiguous at times. But in a plot\nfor children, the stakes had to be clear.
MID_SE_Tia_Set_C_020: I'm sorry, I'm being so blunt after just having read it.\nI haven't really given it enough thought.
MID_SE_Tia_Set_C_021: Oh! No, no...I am the one who should be sorry.\nThat was very childish of me.
MID_SE_Tia_Set_C_022: It's clear that you take your writing seriously, Seteth.
MID_SE_Tia_Set_C_023: And I do think it's to your credit that you seem to\nalways keep the reader in mind.
MID_SE_Tia_Set_C_024: I appreciate that kind of consideration as someone\nwho reads much more than she writes.
MID_SE_Tia_Set_C_025: That is a shame. A reader with your keen insight\nwould write exceptional stories.
MID_SE_Tia_Set_C_026: I'm not so sure about that, but I will think about it.
MID_SE_Tia_Set_C_027: Please, try it. I know you can take on that\nchallenge with aplomb.
MID_SE_Tia_Set_C_028: I'll supply the pen and paper. Then, simply let the\nwriting flow—the way you see the story.
MID_SE_Tia_Set_C_029: That's very kind of you, Seteth. I'll give it a try.
MID_SE_Tia_Set_B: <EDH>
MID_SE_Tia_Set_B_001: "And so, having departed Brigid, he sought to make\nhimself a meal."
MID_SE_Tia_Set_B_002: No, that's not right. He'd already have some food on\nhand. How can I show that he's resourceful, though?
MID_SE_Tia_Set_B_003: How goes the writing, Tialla? Making progress?
MID_SE_Tia_Set_B_004: It's not great. I'm trying to write a grand adventure,\nbut my protagonist is refusing to cooperate.
MID_SE_Tia_Set_B_005: Let me see what you have here... Hmm.
MID_SE_Tia_Set_B_006: I think what you may be struggling with is making\nthis story a little too perfect—a little too tidy.
MID_SE_Tia_Set_B_007: Your protagonist always has the right answer. This\nstory leaves out the flaws and setbacks.
MID_SE_Tia_Set_B_008: It, uh, does seem that way.
MID_SE_Tia_Set_B_009: Maybe try a short story—one that sets your hero\nup for a little drama.
MID_SE_Tia_Set_B_010: Start with the basics. Then build your way to\nwriting epics that are exactly what you want.
MID_SE_Tia_Set_B_011: That's good advice. I'd better practice.
MID_SE_Tia_Set_B_012: I plan on writing a masterpiece that entrances\neveryone who reads it.
MID_SE_Tia_Set_B_013: You set your sights high, I see. Very admirable,\nthough it will make the road ahead longer.
MID_SE_Tia_Set_B_014: I'm well aware of that now. But if I'm doing this, I'm\ndoing it right. It has to be perfect.
MID_SE_Tia_Set_B_015: Hmm. You seem quite set on that. Any reason?
MID_SE_Tia_Set_B_016: A work of literary art is something that tugs at your\nheartstrings. I'd like to leave a story like that behind.
MID_SE_Tia_Set_B_017: So Cai can read it, and remember me.
MID_SE_Tia_Set_B_018: Ah, Cai... He's that friend you've had since you\nwere quite young.
MID_SE_Tia_Set_B_019: Yes. He's the son of Aurora, so he's a demigod who'll\nmost likely live five hundred years, give or take.
MID_SE_Tia_Set_B_020: He'll be around long after I'm dead.
MID_SE_Tia_Set_B_021: Mm...
MID_SE_Tia_Set_B_022: Knowing Cai, he'll make several ordinary lifetimes'\nworth of new friends.
MID_SE_Tia_Set_B_023: But I'd like to think he'll remember me, even if it's\nonly once in a while. A story could help with that.
MID_SE_Tia_Set_B_024: I understand what you're feeling. But, your story\ndoesn't have to be a masterpiece.
MID_SE_Tia_Set_B_025: Writing a story for him to read one day will be\nenough. He'll know you put your heart into it.
MID_SE_Tia_Set_B_026: The one you care for will never forget you.
MID_SE_Tia_Set_B_027: Even after a few hundred years?
MID_SE_Tia_Set_B_028: I promise you he won't. I know—because I have\nlived longer than that.
MID_SE_Tia_Set_B_029: You, Seteth? You're like Cai?
MID_SE_Tia_Set_B_030: Something like that. But, I haven't forgotten a\nsingle soul who was ever important to me.
MID_SE_Tia_Set_B_031: That means the world to me, Seteth. Thank you.
MID_SE_Tia_Set_B_032: Could you... Would you remember me too?
MID_SE_Tia_Set_B_033: Of course, always. I'll store in my heart the days we\nmet—and wrote our stories.